// names.bs -- a name off the wire becomes the atom the VM already has for it,
// or comes back as the reason it has none (F54, ticket 10 §4, ticket 67).
//
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Names Resolve "\"init\""
//         -> :init
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Names Resolve "\"zzz_no_such_name\""
//         -> (:error, "zzz_no_such_name")
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Names Registered "\"init\""
//         -> :true
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Names Registered "\"zzz_no_such_name\""
//         -> :false
//
// THIS IS THE INTEROP ESCAPE, and the reason it exists beside `ParseAtom<T>`
// next door in `examples/Levels`. That one knows the permitted set -- the type
// argument IS the set -- so it never asks the atom table. Here nobody knows
// the set: a peer node's reply, a registered process a client names, a
// handler picked at run time. The only honest question is whether the VM
// already has an atom of that name, and Erlang's safe spelling of that
// question is the long one, `binary_to_existing_atom`, with its `badarg`
// caught. This lowers to exactly that call and nothing else:
//
//     try binary_to_existing_atom(Name, utf8)
//     catch error:badarg -> {error, Name}
//     end
//
// So a string built at run time cannot grow the atom table through this path
// either. What it CAN do is fail, and the failure has to carry which name --
// which is why the result is `result<atom, string>` and not `option<atom>`.
// `atom | :nothing` would collapse to `atom`, because the singleton is
// absorbed into the atom top, and there would be no failure member left to
// match (ticket 15 §1). The tagged member survives.
module Names

using :erlang {
    // `whereis/1` answers a pid or `:undefined`. A pid has no type here, so
    // `term` is the honest declaration: the switch below matches it.
    term whereis(atom name)
}

// Written bare. There is no `<T>` because there is no type to choose: the
// result is fixed. The name stays in the closed set that takes a bracket, so
// `ToExistingAtom<atom>(name)` is refused as the construct's wrong shape
// rather than read as a comparison.
public result<atom, string> Resolve(string name)

Resolve(name) -> ToExistingAtom(name)

// A client asks after a process by name. A name the VM has never seen cannot
// be registered under, so the lookup is only made for a name that resolves --
// and asking did not mint it. The failure is a value, so handling it is an
// arm and not a rescue.
public bool Registered(string name)

Registered(name) -> Resolve(name) switch {
    (:error, _) => false,
    a           => :erlang.whereis(a) switch {
        :undefined => false,
        _          => true
    }
}
